{"hash":"95b5ba8f3855ab59","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":97.986,"said":"You have reached Shri Bhimashankar. Look into the silver basin. The Jyotirlinga is low and broad, barely rising from the stone, and it is not whole: a deep cleft runs through it, so that it seems to be two, Shiva on one side and Shakti on the other. Remember the clay linga in the prison, struck by a demon’s sword, and the Lord who rose from it; remember Shiva and Parvati joined in one form against Tripurasura. Both stories meet in this divided stone, at the head of a river. Fold your hands. The Dwadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram, traditionally attributed to Adi Shankaracharya, sings: ‘Yam Dakini-Shakinika-samaje nishevyamanam pishitashanaish cha, sadaiva Bhimadi-pada-prasiddham tam Shankaram bhaktahitam namami.’ ‘He who is served among the Dakinis and Shakinis, and by fierce spirits, and is ever renowned by the name that begins with Bhima: to that Shankara, who cares for his devotees, I bow.’ The shorter hymn of the twelve names him sixth, ‘Dakinyam Bhimashankaram’. Bhimashankar is the sixth Jyotirlinga of the Stotram. Pilgrims may visit the twelve Jyotirlingas in any order their journey allows; by tradition, the yatra begins at Somnath, the first, and is held complete with darshan at Grishneshwar, the twelfth, near Ellora. Let the bells ring, and let the lamps be raised. Har Har Mahadev.\n\nDid you know? Facing the Jyotirlinga, an image of Parvati stands in the sanctum, looking toward her Lord.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1303},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1319,"end":1410}],"words":[[0.098,0.18,0,3],[0.29,0.24,4,8],[0.543,0.361,9,16],[0.915,0.288,17,21],[1.216,0.841,22,34],[2.957,0.24,36,40],[3.209,0.204,41,45],[3.425,0.084,46,49],[3.522,0.385,50,56],[3.918,0.565,57,62],[5.349,0.132,64,67],[5.493,0.817,68,79],[6.322,0.192,80,82],[6.526,0.216,83,86],[6.755,0.18,87,90],[6.947,0.421,91,96],[7.596,0.373,98,104],[7.981,0.469,105,111],[8.462,0.18,112,116],[8.654,0.